### Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence reveals that there is no uniform federal law in the United States governing the receipt deadlines for mail-in ballots; instead, these rules are determined individually by each state. In states with strict "received-by" deadlines, such as Arizona, Wisconsin, and Florida, mail-in ballots must be received by election officials by Election Day to be counted, meaning late-arriving ballots are discarded regardless of their postmark. However, many other states, including Pennsylvania, Ohio, Washington, and Utah, utilize "postmarked-by" deadlines, which explicitly allow ballots postmarked on or before Election Day to be counted even if they arrive days or weeks after the election. 

### Conclusion
In conclusion, the claim that mail-in ballots postmarked before Election Day but arriving afterward will be thrown out is true only under certain conditions. While this is the case in states with strict "received-by" deadlines, it is entirely false in states that allow postmarked-by extensions. Because the claim presents a rule that varies by jurisdiction as a universal truth, the final decision is `cherry-picking`.

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
Whether a late-arriving mail-in ballot postmarked by Election Day is counted or discarded depends entirely on individual state laws, as there is no federal law mandating their rejection ([Supreme Court filing](https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/20/20-542/158714/20201023203640046_Republican%20Party%20of%20Pennsylvania%20v.%20Boockvar%20-%20Cert%20Petition.pdf)). While states like Arizona, Wisconsin, and Florida enforce strict "received-by" deadlines where late ballots are discarded ([ABC News](https://abcnews.com/Politics/election-2020-fill-mail-ballot-correctly-avoid-rejected/story?id=73484845)), several other states, including Ohio, Pennsylvania, and Washington, explicitly allow ballots postmarked by Election Day to be counted upon late arrival ([BuzzFeed News](https://www.buzzfeednews.com/article/addybaird/vote-by-mail-state-deadlines)). These deadlines and postmark requirements vary widely by jurisdiction and have been subject to specific state court rulings ([Democracy Docket](https://www.democracydocket.com/analysis/mailing-in-your-vote-important-dates/), [NBC News](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/supreme-court/supreme-court-won-t-let-late-mail-ballots-count-wisconsin-n1244865)). Because the claim falsely generalizes a rule that only applies to certain states, the final verdict is cherry-picking.